  "worked_solution": "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}\u20613x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u20613x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u20613x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
